{"content":"⚠️ **IMPORTANT: the bad-merge shadow-duplication is back — ~300 lines of class-level members override main's part-extension implementations**

This PR's `agent_service.dart` (2399 lines vs main's 2051) re-declares at class level a large set of members that main (gh-1232) keeps **only** in the part-file extensions — verified against `origin/main`: `agentNetwork`, `currentSessionId`, `currentSessionCwd`, `hostSecrets`, `subagentManager`, `appLauncher`, `toolsConfig`, `skillsAccess`, `agentModelId`, `sendImage`, `sendToSubagent`, `acceptSecretGrant`, `setSkillsAccess`, `resolveAsrTranscriber`, `beginProviderAddFlow`/`endProviderAddFlow`, the `*ForTest` seams, `_attachApproval`, `_attachRedactor`, `_registerRedactionSecret`, etc. (this setter is one example; `agent_service_wiring.dart:319` still holds the now-dead extension twin of `agentNetwork`).

Dart resolution makes the class copy win, so:

- the part-extension copies from main are **dead code** (nothing warns about this — shadowed extension members compile clean, which is why `flutter analyze` shows 0 errors);
- this PR's own `appLauncher` change (the preflight gate wiring) was applied to the **shadowing class copy**, leaving main's live-by-design extension copy in `agent_service_wiring.dart` stale;
- the rework summary's claim ("the shadowing inline copies are removed so the part-extension implementations are the live ones") does not hold for this file.

The copies are textually identical today, so behavior is currently correct — but this is exactly the silent-divergence landmine the rework said it fixed: any future main change to these members in the part files will merge **without a conflict** and never take effect.

**Fix**: delete the class-level duplicates in `agent_service.dart` (take main's part-file structure), and re-apply the one intentional change — the `openAppTool(env, launcher: launcher, preflight: gate)` wiring — inside `agentServiceWiring`'s `appLauncher` setter in `agent_service_wiring.dart`. A cheap regression guard: a test (or analyze-time convention) asserting none of these member names exist both on the class and in the part extensions.
","path":"outputs/pr_review_comments/comment1_agent_service_shadow_dups.md"}

{"content":"⚠️ **IMPORTANT: a notice whose app is bound to a session with no live `AgentService` is dropped by everyone**

The routing here drops the notice in every live service when `boundId != currentSessionId`, and the fallback (deliver) only fires when the binding is **absent/unreadable/malformed**. There is a third state: the binding names a session that has no live service (user closed the session tab, session deleted from the sidebar, or a CLI/relay host where no `AgentService` exists at all). In that state:

- every live service sees `boundId != currentSessionId` → returns silently;
- the bound session's own service doesn't exist → nobody delivers;
- the notice is not written anywhere durable — no session-tree record, no inbox entry.

The ticket's contract is explicit: error reports are "records in the session tree (no new storage)" and delivery must degrade, "never a silent drop". AC5 (idle delivery to the app-bound session) is only realized when a service for the bound session happens to be alive.

**Fix options**: when `boundId` names a session with no live owner, fall back to writing the bounded notice into that session's tree/inbox for later pickup (the #864 machinery), or deliver via the env's active service with the bound session named in the text. At minimum, cover the closed-bound-session case in `agent_service_js_app_error_delivery_test.dart`.
","path":"outputs/pr_review_comments/comment2_dead_drop_routing.md"}

{"content":"⚠️ **IMPORTANT: the smoke gate boots the app's real JS with its declared permissions on every `open_app` call — load-time network/LLM calls execute (and bill) invisibly**

`smokeProbeEngineFactory` builds a full `JsAppEngine` with `permissions: app.declaredPermissions` and the normal bridge wiring (`fetchHandler`, LLM bridges, storage). That's the right fidelity for catching render errors, but it means the pre-flight run is not side-effect-free:

- an app whose entry (or first-render path) calls `jsr.fetchJson` performs real network requests — including mutating POSTs — each time the agent calls `open_app`, before the user ever sees the app;
- an app that calls `jsr.fa.llm*` on load burns tokens on every gate run, with nothing in the tool result or UI naming that cost;
- the gate adds up-to-10s engine boot latency to every `open_app` invocation (repeated opens re-run it — no cache/pass memo per revision).

**Fix**: add a probe mode (e.g. `probeMode: true` on `JsAppEngine`, used only by this factory) that stubs `jsr.fetchJson`/LLM bridges to a named "preflight stub" answer — the app still renders (first render + timers still exercised), but network/LLM side effects can't fire, and the gate outcome can state "bridges stubbed". Alternatively document the side-effect contract explicitly in `runAppPreflight`'s doc and the SKILL.md pre-flight section so app authors know load-time fetch/LLM runs twice per open.
","path":"outputs/pr_review_comments/comment3_smoke_gate_side_effects.md"}

{"content":"💡 **SUGGESTION: make `user-invocable: true` explicit in the frontmatter**

`/js-apps` works today only because `user-invocable` currently **defaults to true** in `SkillManifest` (`skill_manifest.dart:217`). The ticket pins `/js-apps` as an AC (AC1), and the other builtin (`self-settings`) sets the flag explicitly. If the manifest default ever flips (e.g. to mirror Claude's opt-in semantics), `/js-apps` silently de-lists with no test failing — `builtin_js_apps_skill_test.dart` asserts `userInvocable` is true but would keep passing only as long as the default holds; an explicit flag makes the AC structural. Consider also adding `when_to_use:` (the auto-suggestion trigger the ticket asks for: "user asks for JS app/widget work") — today only the free-text `description` drives suggestion.
","path":"outputs/pr_review_comments/comment5_skill_frontmatter.md"}

